        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Under-Desk Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages and
            specification tags, published hands-on reviews, warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the two things the searches behind this page ask for — a seated elliptical
            and an under-desk bike — and looked for models on sale on 19 October 2026, sold on Amazon
            US, and described by their maker in enough detail to check against a desk. We then looked
            for independent coverage. Garage Gym Reviews has used and written about the Cubii line, the
            DeskCycle 2 and Sunny's low-profile magnetic mini bike, and those three cards carry figures
            that both the maker and that coverage support. The DeskCycle's own site did not load on the
            day, so its desk-height guidance is quoted as DeskCycle's, as reported by Garage Gym
            Reviews.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two of Sunny's seated ellipticals are carded even though we found no independent hands-on
            review of either. We included them because seated ellipticals are what most buyers are
            looking for, Sunny publishes unusually complete specifications — including the maximum pedal
            height, which most makers omit — and Sunny is an established brand with a recall-free record
            for these products. Treat their figures as Sunny's claims.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls and exclusions</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the CPSC database by maker name and in recall titles for Cubii and its maker
            Fitness Cubed, DeskCycle and its maker 3D Innovations, Sunny Health &amp; Fitness, LifePro,
            ANCHEER and other common pedaler brands, and searched recall titles for pedal exercisers,
            ellipticals and under-desk products. We found no recall of any under-desk elliptical or
            pedal exerciser. Two brands buyers often see in this category do have CPSC recalls, of
            different products: LifePro recalled <a href="/best-home-saunas/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>infrared sauna</a> blankets in October 2025, and e-bikes
            distributed by ANCHEER were recalled in October 2022. Neither recall concerns a pedaler,
            but we left both brands off this page because we could not load LifePro's store or find an
            ANCHEER maker page to check a current model's figures against.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also passed over models we could confirm from only one side. Cubii's site lists the Cubii
            Go, with wheels, a retractable handle and 10 resistance levels, and the Cubii Total Body,
            with 12 levels and an upper-body pulley system, but publishes no dimensions for either, and
            we found no independent figures to check. The MagneTrainer-ER, an under-desk bike Garage Gym
            Reviews recommends for offices at about $180, stands about 16 inches tall by its reviewer's
            account — on the tall side for a low desk — and we could not reach its maker's site to
            confirm the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clearance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Desk Height and Knee Clearance: Measure Before You Buy</h2>
<p>
            This is the section that decides whether an under-desk elliptical works for you. The device
            has to fit under the desk, and — the part people forget — your knees have to stay below the
            underside of the desk at the highest point of every stroke. Knees that bump the desk, or a
            keyboard tray, a drawer or a crossbar, end the habit within a week.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 1: measure the gap, not the desk</h3>
          <p>
            Measure from the floor to the lowest thing under the desk where your knees will be: the
            underside of the desktop, a front apron or modesty panel, a drawer, or a keyboard tray. That
            number is often an inch or more below the height of the desk surface. Many fixed desks have a
            top surface somewhere around 28 to 30 inches, so the real clearance is usually in the mid to
            high 20s.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 2: find your knee height at the top of the stroke</h3>
          <p>
            Sit in your chair at your normal working height. Put one foot on a box, a stack of books or
            anything that brings your sole to the machine's highest pedal position, then measure from the
            floor to the top of that knee. Add your shoes. That is the height your knee reaches at the top
            of each stroke. It needs to be below the clearance from step 1, with an inch or two to spare.
          </p>
          <p>
            Makers rarely publish the highest pedal position, which is why we favour the ones that do.
            Sunny gives the SF-E323017's pedal step height as 3.4 to 6.9 inches and its motorized
            SF-E3959SMART as 3.35 to 10.9 inches — a 4-inch difference in how high your foot rises. A
            pedal bike lifts your foot further, because its pedals turn a full circle: DeskCycle sets the
            DeskCycle 2's pedals at 9 or 10 inches off the floor on its adjustable-height version, which
            Garage Gym Reviews' tester said was low compared with other mini bikes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the makers say about desk height</h3>
          <p>
            DeskCycle is the one maker in this group that publishes desk-height guidance: according to
            Garage Gym Reviews, DeskCycle says the DeskCycle 2 works at a desk as low as 27 inches for
            people up to about 5 feet 8, and at 28 inches for people up to about 5 feet 11. Treat that
            as a starting point rather than a guarantee; your leg proportions and chair matter as much as
            your height.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If your desk is too low</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Choose a seated elliptical over a bike.</strong> The flatter elliptical path lifts
              the knee less.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower the chair</strong> an inch, if you can still type comfortably, or sit
              further back so your knees are not under the desk's front edge.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the keyboard tray</strong> or move the drawer unit to one side.</li>
            <li><strong>Raise a sit-stand desk</strong> an inch or two while you pedal and raise your
              chair to match, keeping your elbows at about desk height. Our guide to <a href="/standing-desk-height-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standing desk height for a walking pad</a> covers the elbow-height rule, and our <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a> list desks with a wide height range.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="chair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Stopping the Chair Rolling Away</h2>
<p>
            Every push on a pedal also pushes you, and your chair, backwards. On an office chair with
            casters on a hard floor or a smooth chair mat, that means the chair creeps away a little with
            every stroke and the machine creeps forward. It is the second most common complaint about
            under-desk pedalers after knee clearance, and it is easy to fix before it starts.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Use a chair that does not roll</strong>, or one with locking casters. A kitchen
              or dining chair at the right height works well, if it supports your back.</li>
            <li><strong>Put the chair on carpet or a rug</strong>, which resists rolling, but put the
              pedaler on something firm. Garage Gym Reviews notes the Cubii Move may wobble on carpet; a
              thin, firm mat or board under the machine helps.</li>
            <li><strong>Tether the machine to the chair.</strong> Some pedalers come with a strap that
              loops round a chair leg. Garage Gym Reviews found the strap on one cheap pedaler it tried
              flimsy, so check the buckle, or use a sturdier strap of your own.</li>
            <li><strong>Brace the machine</strong> against a wall, the desk's modesty panel or a heavy
              object, and use the non-slip feet or floor stabilisers if it has them.</li>
            <li><strong>Pedal more lightly.</strong> High resistance makes every push bigger. At a desk,
              lighter resistance at a steady pace is usually the better choice anyway.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A chair that keeps your thighs roughly level, with your feet able to reach the pedals at full
            extension without stretching, is also the comfortable one. If you have to perch on the front
            edge of the seat to reach the pedals, the machine is too far away or the chair too high.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="elliptical-vs-bike" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Under Desk Elliptical vs Under Desk Bike</h2>
<p>
            The two look similar and suit different desks and different people.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A seated elliptical</strong> moves your feet in a flat oval, as a full-size elliptical
            does, with your feet resting on long footplates. The knee rises less, so it fits under lower
            desks, and the motion is easy to keep going while you concentrate. Resistance is usually
            gentle. Cubii's and Sunny's seated ellipticals are designed to be used only while seated;
            Garage Gym Reviews notes that you should not stand on the Cubii.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An under-desk bike, or pedal exerciser,</strong> turns your feet in a circle like a
            bicycle. The foot travels higher, so it needs more knee room, but bikes generally offer more
            resistance — DeskCycle says the DeskCycle 2 reaches about 39 lb, and Garage Gym Reviews'
            tester found its top level genuinely hard. Many, like the Sunny SF-B0891SMART, can also sit on
            a desk or table so you can pedal with your hands.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: <strong>if your desk is low or you want something you barely notice, choose
            an elliptical; if your desk has room and you want more work, choose a bike.</strong> If you
            want to use it away from the desk as a gentle workout in front of the television, either
            works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Noise and Resistance Levels</h2>
<p>
            We have not measured the noise of any machine here, and we do not repeat makers' decibel
            claims. What independent use reports is reassuring: Garage Gym Reviews' tester called the
            DeskCycle 2 virtually silent and said she could pedal through work calls unnoticed, and found
            Sunny's low-profile mini bike quiet apart from some minor clicking with each stroke.
          </p>
          <p>
            All five machines here use <strong>magnetic resistance</strong> or a motor, which is the
            quiet choice. Cheap pedalers that use a friction strap or felt pad against the flywheel can
            squeak and wear, and their resistance changes as the pad warms. Motorized machines add the
            sound of a motor; Sunny calls the SF-E3959SMART's quiet, and we have no independent account
            either way.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Resistance levels are not comparable between brands.</strong> Six levels on a Cubii,
            eight on a Sunny and eight on a DeskCycle describe each maker's own scale. What matters at a
            desk is the bottom of the range — a level light enough to keep going for an hour while you
            type — and enough steps above it for a harder session after work. Garage Gym Reviews' tester
            found the top of Sunny's mini bike range not very demanding and the top of the DeskCycle's
            hard, which is the best comparison we have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Calorie Numbers on an Under-Desk Elliptical Mean</h2>
<p>
            Every machine on this page shows a calorie figure, and several listings use calorie burn to
            sell them. Treat those numbers with the same caution as a treadmill console's. Our guide to <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmill calories are accurate</a> explains why consoles tend to run high, and an under-desk machine has less to go on than a
            treadmill: it rarely knows your weight, it cannot see how hard you are pushing beyond the
            pedal speed and level, and it does not know whether you are pedalling gently or bracing
            against the chair.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here is the arithmetic, so you can see the scale. Energy use is commonly estimated from METs,
            where 1 MET is your resting rate; our <a href="/what-are-mets-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to METs</a> explains the standard formula: calories per minute = METs x 3.5 x body weight in kg / 200.
            Suppose, purely for illustration, that easy seated pedalling costs twice your resting rate, 2
            METs. For a 176 lb (80 kg) person that is 2 x 3.5 x 80 / 200 = 2.8 calories a minute, or
            about 168 an hour. But about 84 of those you would have used sitting still, so the <strong>extra</strong> energy from an hour of pedalling is closer to 84 calories.
          </p>
          <p>
            That illustration is an estimate built on an assumed figure, not a measurement, and your own
            number will differ. Two lessons hold regardless. First, the gross figure on a console includes
            calories you would have burned anyway. Second, a motorized machine that moves your legs for
            you does less work than one you pedal yourself, so treat its calorie figure as the least
            reliable of all. An under-desk elliptical is a good way to break up sitting and add gentle
            movement; it is not a substitute for exercise that raises your heart rate, and it will not
            move the scale much on its own. Our guide to <a href="/do-walking-pads-help-you-lose-weight/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether walking pads help you lose weight</a> makes the same point about desk walking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-desk-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Under-Desk Elliptical, Bike or Walking Pad?</h2>
<p>
            If your aim is to move more during the working day, there are three main options, and the
            right one depends on your desk and how you work.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Option</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Desk needed</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical cost</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Best for</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Under-desk elliptical</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Your current desk, if the knees clear</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $170 to $270 for the picks here</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Gentle, near-constant movement while seated</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Under-desk bike</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Your current desk, with more knee room</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $140 to $160 for the picks here</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">More resistance; arm use on a table</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking pad</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A standing desk that rises high enough</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">See our walking pad guides</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">More movement and energy use; weight-bearing</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Walking uses more energy than seated pedalling and loads your bones, but it needs a desk that
            reaches well above your seated height plus the deck, a power socket and more floor space.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-desk-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk guide</a> covers setting one up, the <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a> cover the desk itself, and if noise is the concern, our <a href="/best-quiet-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet walking pads</a> guide looks at the pads easiest to live with. Plenty of people end up alternating: a walking
            pad for calls and reading, a seated pedaler for focused typing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Using an Under-Desk Elliptical Comfortably</h2>
<p>
            A few habits make the difference between a machine you use every day and one that ends up
            in a cupboard.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start with short blocks.</strong> Ten to twenty minutes at a time, a few times a
              day, is easier on the knees and hips than an hour straight on the first day.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the resistance low while you work.</strong> Save the higher levels for a
              session when you are not typing.</li>
            <li><strong>Sit back.</strong> Keep your back against the chair rather than perching forward,
              and move the machine until your knee stays slightly bent at the furthest point of the
              stroke.</li>
            <li><strong>Wear shoes</strong>, or at least socks with grip, and use the straps if the
              pedals have them.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the cables.</strong> A battery-powered display needs nothing, but a
              motorized or app-connected machine may need a power lead; route it away from your feet and
              the chair's wheels.</li>
            <li><strong>Stop if it hurts.</strong> Knee or hip pain beyond ordinary tiredness is a sign
              to shorten the sessions or check the set-up, and to ask a professional if it persists.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want real cardio sessions,</strong> an under-desk machine is too light. A full
            exercise bike or elliptical is the better buy; see the <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best exercise bikes</a>,
            the <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best recumbent bikes</a> if you want a supported seat, or the <a href="/best-elliptical-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best elliptical machines for home</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already have a standing desk that rises high enough,</strong> a walking pad
            will give you more movement for the same desk time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your desk clearance is under about 27 inches</strong> and cannot be changed, you
            may struggle with any of these, especially a bike. Measure first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are buying for rehabilitation or for an older relative,</strong> ask the
            physiotherapist or doctor involved what kind of movement and resistance is appropriate, and
            whether a motorized machine is suitable, before choosing.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the rest of the room, our <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym hub</a> and <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best cardio machines for home</a> cover the bigger machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying an Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
          <p>Almost every disappointment with an under-desk machine comes down to one of these four.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring the desktop instead of the gap</h3>
              <p>The height that matters is the lowest thing under the desk — the underside, an apron, a drawer or a keyboard tray — not the work surface. Measure that, then check your knee height at the machine's highest pedal position. A desk that looks tall enough often is not once the tray is counted.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing a bike for a low desk</h3>
              <p>A pedal bike turns your feet in a full circle and lifts the knee higher than a seated elliptical. If your clearance is tight, an elliptical with a published pedal height is the safer buy, and a motorized model with higher pedal travel is the riskiest.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the chair</h3>
              <p>An office chair on casters will roll back with every push. Plan for a chair that stays put, locking casters, a rug, or a tether before the machine arrives, or the first week will be spent chasing your chair across the room.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Believing the calorie readout</h3>
              <p>The number on the screen is an estimate, usually without your weight, and includes calories you would have used sitting still. Use the machine to move more during the day, and judge progress by how you feel and how much you use it, not by the console.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most people, the Cubii Move is the under desk elliptical to
            buy, and the DeskCycle 2 is the under desk bike</strong> if your desk has the room. Sunny's
            models are the budget alternatives, with unusually complete published specifications, and its
            motorized elliptical suits only people who want assisted movement and have the knee room.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before you order, measure the gap under the desk and your knee height at the top of the
            stroke, and decide how you will stop the chair rolling. Then treat the calorie number as an
            estimate. If you have a standing desk, compare with a walking pad first — <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our under-desk treadmill guide</a> covers the walking route.
          </p>
        </div>

