        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best under desk elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the Cubii Move: a low-profile seated elliptical with six resistance levels for about $200, sold through Cubii's Amazon store. If you want an app and published dimensions for less, Sunny's SF-E323017 is a budget alternative, though its figures are Sunny's own and we found no independent review.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best under desk bike?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The DeskCycle 2. Its adjustable-height version puts the pedals 9 or 10 inches off the floor, among the lowest of any under-desk bike, it has eight magnetic levels, and an independent tester found it virtually silent. Sunny's low-profile magnetic mini bike is a cheaper, more portable option with lighter resistance.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much clearance do you need for an under desk elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Your knee at the top of the stroke must stay below the lowest thing under the desk, with an inch or two to spare. Sit at your working height, raise one foot to the machine's highest pedal position, measure to the top of your knee and compare it with the floor-to-underside measurement. Seated ellipticals need less room than bikes.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do under desk ellipticals really burn calories?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some, but less than the screen suggests. Console figures are estimates and include calories you would burn sitting still. As an illustration, if easy pedalling cost 2 METs, an 80 kg person would use about 168 calories an hour in total but only about 84 more than sitting. It helps break up sitting; it is not a substitute for exercise.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I stop my chair rolling when using an under desk elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Use a chair without casters or with locking casters, put the chair on a rug or carpet, tether the machine to a chair leg with a sturdy strap, or brace it against a wall or the desk. Lower resistance also reduces the push. Keep the machine itself on a firm surface so it does not wobble.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is an under desk elliptical better than a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on your desk. An under-desk elliptical works at the desk you already have, if your knees clear, and suits focused typing. A walking pad needs a standing desk that rises high enough, but gives more movement and energy use and is weight-bearing. Many people use both at different times of day.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

